  "title": "Quadratic convergence of SOS-density bounds on convex bodies",
  "description": "<p>For every fixed compact convex body K in R^n with nonempty interior and every fixed real polynomial f, the normalized Lebesgue global sum-of-squares density hierarchy of degree at most 2r has error at most C(K,f)/r^2 for every integer r at least 1. A self-contained elementary proof gives an explicit constant and a single global square on the whole body. A quadratic Taylor upper proxy, homothetic sublevel growth, and a fixed power of an endpoint Dirichlet polynomial yield a normalized first-moment bound without a logarithmic loss. This proves the quadratic upper rate in the convex-body/Lebesgue regime of OWR-16633-006, with the probability-density unit-mass convention explicitly restored. The known interval linear example shows sharpness of the worst-case exponent; no positive matching lower rate for every objective is asserted.</p><p>The quadratic proxy, endpoint localization, and broad relative shell/core mass mechanism have substantial classical precedents, including de Klerk-Laurent, Slot-Laurent, and Totik-Varga. A deduction from older endpoint-polynomial theory was assembled during this audit; that does not show that an older source printed the complete target theorem. The bounded priority search located no earlier explicit full-target theorem in the inspected passages, and makes no claim of firstness, a new method, disclosure lineage, or current worldwide openness. Arbitrary reference measures, the restricted original-objective pushforward hierarchy for general nonconvex objectives, and an efficient method to find the minimizer are outside the result.</p><p>The deposit includes the note and a portable TeX-source, complete-proof, exact-arithmetic diagnostic, independent AI adversarial-review, integrity-verification, and bounded-priority archive. Numerical spectral stress tests are empirical, not proof certificates. Finite exact checks do not certify the infinite theorem. AI tools were used extensively in solving, derivations, source review, verification, drafting, and adversarial reviews. Unrefereed; no external human peer review, journal acceptance, or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed.</p>",
